      "id": "s1",
      "nodeId": "n1",
      "text": "ChatGPT with GPT-4 uses approximately 519 milliliters of water, slightly more than one 16.9 ounce bottle, in order to write one 100-word email, according to original research from The Washington Post and the University of California, Riverside.",
      "contextBefore": "e Equivalent of Consuming One Bottle of Water. ChatGPT usage can draw as much water as the state of Rhode Island does in 1.5 days, say The Washington Post and the University of California, Riverside. ",
      "contextAfter": " This extravagant resource use can worsen human-caused drought conditions, particularly in already dry climates. The Washington Post's reporting is based on the research paper \"Making AI Less \"Thirsty",
      "start": 236,
      "end": 480,
      "extractSha256": "7815a52cf6c0a09637e98ece22940a6377b0e4378e74e2647de9a3783a03abbe",
      "windowSha256": "eb8b9da844e4fa01ed684c4d37bdb1c6d99a0b0e8443b4a2235636743185f8f0",
      "fetchedAt": "2026-11-03T02:32:38.194Z",
      "waybackUrl": "https://web.archive.org/web/20240920194929/https://www.techrepublic.com/article/generative-ai-data-center-water-use",
      "tier": "exact"
    },
    {
      "id": "s2",
      "nodeId": "n2",
      "text": "While the exact burden is nearly impossible to quantify, The Washington Post worked with researchers at the University of California, Riverside to understand how much water and power OpenAI's ChatGPT, using the GPT-4 language model released in March 2023, consumes to write the average 100-word email. ---",
      "contextBefore": "uery exacts a cost. Chatbots use an immense amount of power to respond to user questions, and simply keeping the bot's servers cool enough to function in data centers takes a toll on the environment. ",
      "contextAfter": "",
      "start": 581,
      "end": 886,
      "extractSha256": "0802dddc0fcdbf69f841d427d00b927fb73a5dffc0d5ab6f880656eac66daaee",
      "windowSha256": "bd8f1464eb68f586be42967df76c7760feecc4446b8522f064056565627f76b7",
      "fetchedAt": "2026-11-03T02:32:38.504Z",
      "waybackUrl": "https://web.archive.org/web/20240918110626/https://www.washingtonpost.com/technology/2024/09/18/energy-ai-use-electricity-water-data-centers",
      "tier": "exact"
    },
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "nodeId": "n3",
      "text": "A typical text prompt's cooling water is a few milliliters or less; lifecycle estimates reach about a bottle. * Google's official figure: a median Gemini text prompt uses about 0.26 mL of water, roughly five drops, counting on-site cooling. * The viral figure: roughly 519 mL (about one bottle) to write a 100-word email with GPT-4, because it also counts the electricity supply chain. * The 1,000x gap is about what you count: on-site cooling alone versus the full lifecycle. * Bigger models drink more.",
      "contextBefore": "ly deserves your attention, which is not the one on your screen. Every figure here is dated and attributed. Press enter or click to view image in full size Key Takeaways. * There is no single number. ",
      "contextAfter": " Heavy reasoning models use far more energy, and water scales with energy. * The real story is scale and location, not your individual prompt. US data centers used about 17.4 billion gallons directly ",
      "start": 1472,
      "end": 1976,
      "extractSha256": "ded0048c642b3b33cc7426746ef8475cf6d0285a33f02c943e31f048b70c55d8",
      "windowSha256": "753e1151e29167399ee467695ee0e12509cc257ab705c5ffbb9f19f3f5f7a369",
      "fetchedAt": "2026-11-03T02:32:39.006Z",
      "waybackUrl": "https://web.archive.org/web/20260703052351/https://medium.com/@macplanet2012/how-much-water-does-ai-use-per-prompt-5-drops-or-a-bottle-d38c90f22122",
      "tier": "exact"
    },
